WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.800
 So like I said, you should go visit CERN. I have had the great fortune to visit CERN.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:13.020
 I have visited CERN in a professional role. I helped them record lectures with my Synch-O-Matic

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:21.200
 software that you may have heard me talk about. I have visited sort of in helping other technology

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:26.720
 things and teaching and learning with technology and things like that. And so they've got a

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:31.560
 wonderful cafe. And if you are working with people, you get to go in the back and hang

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:37.320
 out. That's pretty cool. They also have a wonderful museum that you can go see the first

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:44.940
 web server and all kinds of other cool stuff. And in 2006, '06, I was lucky enough to have

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:50.640
 an invited talk at CERN where I talked about Sakai and how they might use Sakai to do collaborative

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:56.640
 work. And I brought my family with me. And so my wife, Teresa, and my daughter, Mandy,

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:03.000
 and my son, Brent, and there is me. We are in the pit. And this pit is like eight or

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.640
 nine stories underground. It is six stories tall. This is where the beam comes in right

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:14.280
 here. It's three stories from the bottom of the pit. The pit is six stories tall. At this

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:19.800
 point, it was only less than one third complete. And so we could go on a tour. And so I have

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:26.760
 a family photo with hard hats in the CERN pit. And that's pretty cool. Another time

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:31.900
 I visited, I went and sang with one of our University of Michigan physicists. And that

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:36.060
 would be this guy right here. His name is Stephen Goldfarb. And he's a physicist that

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:42.960
 works on the Atlas project. But he also is the band leader of an all-physicist band called

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:50.760
 the Cernets, the Cernets Blues Band. And he let me sing a song. I was coming to do some

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:56.480
 video work for him. I happened to be in the area. And I just stopped by on one of my trips.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:01.560
 And me and another Michigan staffer, we grabbed a couple of cameras. We made some music videos

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:05.680
 for him and put the Cernets, some of the Cernets music videos up on the web. And then they

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.760
 let me sing one song called "Got My Mojo Workin'." And so I've got, I'll share with

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:16.160
 you the video of "Got My Mojo Workin'." It's just, oh, so everybody in this band is

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:21.400
 a physicist and pretty much everybody dancing is a physicist too. Now the reason I'm showing

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:27.720
 you all this is to give you a sense of the energy and joy in addition to the hard work

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:41.320
